rse adds the coordinate inclusions c -> c e_i. On pure tensors their two composites are the identity, hence on every tensor by linearity.\", \"uses\": [], \"objects\": [\"T\", \"mu\", \"J\", \"R\", \"iota\", \"M_R\", \"I\", \"e\"], \"uses_local\": [\"F001\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F002\", \"statement\": \"T=Q tensor_R J is finite dimensional over Q; mu:T->C, q tensor c |-> qc, is injective; and Q tensor_R M_R is Q-linearly isomorphic to the finite product T^I.\", \"objects\": [\"T\", \"mu\", \"J\", \"R\", \"M_R\", \"I\"], \"binders\": [], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S003\", \"text\": \"Set r=dim_Q T, a finite natural number by F002. Apply M002 with source B=R, A=Q and source module M_R. N002 provides its free R-basis of n elements, so finrank_R M_R=n and M002 gives dim_Q(Q tensor_R M_R)=n. The rings R and Q are commutative domains and satisfy the strong rank condition: for R, clear denominators to preserve injectivity after passage to its fraction field Q, then apply finite-dimensional linear algebra over Q; for Q use that same dimension inequality directly. Apply M001 over Q to the constant family T indexed by I. F002 supplies finite dimensionality and hence a finite basis of T, while N001 supplies |I|=n. Thus dim_Q(T^I)=sum_(i in I)r=nr. The isomorphism in F002 now gives n=nr; n>0 by N001, whence r=1. Together with injectivity of mu, J therefore spans a one-dimensional rational line in C.\", \"uses\": [\"N002\", \"N001\", \"M002\", \"M001\"], \"objects\": [\"r\", \"T\", \"mu\", \"R\", \"M_R\", \"n\", \"I\", \"J\"], \"uses_local\": [\"F002\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F003\", \"statement\": \"dim_Q(Q tensor_R J)=1, mu is injective, and the Q-linear span of J in C is one-dimensional.\", \"objects\": [\"J\", \"R\", \"mu\"], \"binders\": [], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S004\", \"text\": \"F001 and F003 imply existence of a nonzero a in J and a nonempty finite list of non